const automationAuditBody = `
<p>A business automation audit maps where your practice is losing time to manual work, then ranks every fix by what it is worth and how fast it pays back. It is not a workshop. It is not a course. It ends with a costed, day-by-day plan, not a slide deck.</p>

<p>Most businesses that try to "do AI" fail within six months. Not because the tools don't work. According to <a href="https://hbr.org/2025/11/most-ai-initiatives-fail-this-5-part-framework-can-help" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Harvard Business Review</a>, most AI initiatives fail not because the models are weak, but because organisations aren't built to sustain them. The tools were never the missing piece. The diagnosis was.</p>

<p>That's what an audit is for. This article covers what it involves, what it costs, who it's for, and why R5,497 buys more certainty than a free call ever will.</p>

<h2>What a business automation audit actually is</h2>

<p>Strip away the AI framing and it's a simple thing: a structured look at how your practice actually spends its hours, and a ranked plan for fixing the parts that waste the most of them.</p>

<p>It maps your real workflows, not generic ones. Lead handling. Client communication. Reporting. Billing. Compliance filing. Every task gets scored on an Impact-Effort basis: how much time or money it's costing you, versus how hard it is to fix.</p>

<p>The output isn't a list of AI tools to try. It's a ranked sequence: fix this first, this second, this third, and here's roughly what each one is worth in rands per year.</p>

<p>We call this the Business Automation Assessment. It's the same category of work the search term "business automation audit" describes, just under Cobus's own name. If you've searched that term, you're likely already past the "is AI even relevant to my practice" question and into "what does this actually involve, and is it worth paying for."</p>

<p>That distinction matters because most of what a professional services practice loses time to isn't glamorous. It's a partner manually copying figures from a bank statement into a spreadsheet because the reconciliation tool was never set up properly. It's a paralegal retyping the same letter of engagement with three fields changed. It's an advisor re-explaining the same FAIS disclosure by hand in every intake meeting because there's no template that auto-populates. None of that needs a chatbot. It needs someone to notice it, cost it, and sequence a fix. That noticing is the audit.</p>

<h2>What happens during the audit, step by step</h2>

<p>Before any of it, there's a free 5-minute scorecard — a short quiz that qualifies fit and shows you roughly where your practice sits, at no cost and no commitment. It's a lead magnet, not part of the audit itself. The paid audit only starts once you've decided to go ahead, and it runs in four stages from there:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>90-minute intake call.</strong> Not generic questions. We map your actual workflows: how a lead becomes a client, how a report gets built, how billing happens, what compliance touches every file.</li>
  <li><strong>Analysis phase (5 business days).</strong> The analysis structures everything into an Impact-Effort matrix, ranked by rand value and setup difficulty.</li>
  <li><strong>Full written report.</strong> Every automation opportunity found, ranked, with specific tool recommendations, ZAR costs, setup times, and a day-by-day implementation sequence.</li>
  <li><strong>30-minute debrief call.</strong> A walkthrough of the findings together, not a report dropped in your inbox with no explanation.</li>
