<p>None of that shows up in a generic questionnaire. It shows up when someone asks "walk me through what happened the last time you onboarded a client" and listens for where the friction actually is.</p>

<h2>What you actually get at the end</h2>

<p>Five things, specifically:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A straight answer to "how should I use AI in my business?"</strong> Not a trend list or a tool roundup — your own answer, specific to your practice, in priority order: what to do first, what to leave alone, and why.</li>
  <li><strong>A ranked list of automation opportunities</strong> specific to your practice, not a generic industry list.</li>
  <li><strong>Tool recommendations that work in South Africa.</strong> SA banking integrations, SARS-compatible filing, WhatsApp Business API, POPIA-compliant data handling — not a US SaaS list that assumes Stripe and Slack.</li>
  <li><strong>A day-by-day implementation plan</strong> a team member or a contractor can pick up and execute without needing you to explain it.</li>
  <li><strong>A rand number.</strong> The audit is built to find a minimum of R50,000 in annual savings. If it doesn't, the fee is refunded — see the guarantee below.</li>
</ul>

<p>This is the piece most audits from bigger consulting shops skip: SA-specific tool matching. A generic AI-readiness report written for a US mid-market firm will recommend tools that don't talk to SARS e-filing or don't have a WhatsApp integration at all. That gap is where a lot of the real Information Gain on this topic sits, and it's largely absent from what currently ranks for this search term.</p>

<p>In practice, that means the report names actual categories your practice already touches, matched to what that specific practice type runs on:</p>

