<p>Not because any specific tool is being pushed, but because the fix has to work with the systems your practice already runs on, not a stack it doesn't have.</p>

<h2>Who this is for (and who it isn't)</h2>

<p><strong>This is for:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>SA professional services firms and agencies — accountants, attorneys, financial advisors, marketing agencies — anywhere a lot of time is lost to manual, repetitive computer work</li>
  <li>Companies with 3 to 50 staff</li>
  <li>Owners, directors, and CEOs who already feel the friction: the Monday catch-up, the same spreadsheet, the same manual reconciliation every month-end</li>
  <li>The ambitious employee too: a COO, CMO, or director who wants to get ahead of AI in their own role, not just fix the business's processes — sharper skills, better work quality, real time back</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>This isn't for:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Tech startups (different problem, and not enough repeatable manual process to find R50,000 in)</li>
  <li>Businesses under 3 staff (not enough process volume yet to justify the fee)</li>
</ul>

<p>If you're an accountant billing R1,500 an hour and still typing meeting notes by hand at 9pm, that's the profile this was built for. The audit assumes you're busy, not that you have spare hours to sit through a course on prompt engineering. It's built around the practice you already have, not the one a workshop imagines you might build.</p>

<h2>Why R5,497 and not a free call</h2>

<p>The guarantee answers this directly: if the audit doesn't find R50,000 in annual savings, you pay nothing.</p>

<p>Here's the maths behind why that's a low bar, not a marketing line. At a conservative R800 an hour, recovering just 10 hours a week of admin time returns the R5,497 fee in under a week. Most practices we've mapped are losing more than 10 hours a week to manual work across the whole team, not one person.</p>

<p>A free call can't produce that number. A free call is 20 minutes of conversation. It can't map five workflows, cost each fix in rands, and sequence an implementation plan — that takes the 90-minute intake, the 5-day analysis window, and a written deliverable. The fee funds the actual analysis time, not a sales conversation dressed up as one.</p>

<p>As an illustration of the maths, not a claim about a specific client: a two-partner attorney practice billing R2,000 an hour, where each partner loses roughly 6 hours a week to matter administration that a paraprofessional or a properly scoped automation could handle, is sitting on close to R1.2 million a year in recoverable capacity across the practice. The audit doesn't need to find anything close to that scale to clear the R50,000 guarantee. It needs to find about 4% of it.</p>

<p>There's also an international price anchor worth knowing. The same category of diagnostic — an AI or automation readiness audit — runs <a href="https://ariesconsultinggroup.com/blog/ai-readiness-audit-cost/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">$5,000 to $50,000-plus in the US market</a>, depending on business size: $5,000 to $15,000 for a mid-market firm, $15,000 to $50,000-plus for anything enterprise-scale. At current exchange rates, R5,497 works out to roughly $300. The price isn't an accident. It's set to make the decision easy for an SA practice this size, not to match what a Big Four consultancy would charge for the same category of work.</p>

<blockquote>The assessment doesn't cost R5,497. It finds R50,000. The report is how you see exactly where it is.</blockquote>

<h2>Why "just use ChatGPT" misses the point</h2>

<p>ChatGPT, Copilot, and a dozen automation platforms are already available to every business owner reading this. Availability was never the constraint. Cost isn't either — most of these tools are R300 to R1,000 a month.</p>

<p>The real question is which of your specific workflows they'll actually improve, in what order, and what breaks if you sequence it wrong. A tool pointed at the wrong process doesn't save time. It just moves the manual work somewhere less visible, or creates a new problem — a client communication that goes out with the wrong tone, a filing that skips a compliance step nobody flagged.</p>

<p>This is where a lot of businesses that "already tried AI" get stuck. They bought Copilot or ChatGPT Plus, someone used it for a week to draft emails, and then it quietly stopped being used because nobody sequenced it against an actual workflow. The tool wasn't wrong. It was pointed at the wrong first problem, with no plan for what came after week one.</p>

<blockquote>Most AI projects fail because of bad diagnosis, not bad tools.</blockquote>

<p>That's the finding behind the HBR framework cited above, and it's consistent with what we see auditing SA practices directly. The tool is the last decision in this process, not the first. The audit exists to make that decision correctly, once, instead of by trial and error six times.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>What does an AI readiness audit involve?</h3>
<p>A 90-minute intake call mapping your real workflows, five business days of analysis turning that into a ranked Impact-Effort matrix, a full written report with specific tools and ZAR costs, and a 30-minute debrief call. No generic questionnaire — the questions are shaped around your actual practice.</p>

<h3>How long does an AI readiness assessment take?</h3>
<p>About a week from the intake call to the finished report. The 5-minute scorecard beforehand qualifies fit; it doesn't count toward the audit timeline itself.</p>

<h3>What is the ROI of a business automation audit?</h3>
<p>The audit is built to find a minimum of R50,000 in annual savings, guaranteed. At a conservative R800-an-hour billing rate, recovering 10 hours a week of admin time alone returns the R5,497 fee in under a week.</p>

<h3>What is the difference between an AI audit and AI consulting?</h3>
<p>A business automation audit is a fixed-scope diagnostic: map the workflows, rank the fixes, hand over a plan, done in about a week. Consulting is ongoing, ad hoc, and open-ended, billed by the hour or the month with no fixed endpoint. The audit is the first step, and it's designed to make the consulting decision unnecessary for anyone who just wants the plan and can implement it themselves. It tells you exactly what to fix, in what order, before you pay anyone, including us, to help implement it.</p>

<h3>How do I know if my business needs an AI audit?</h3>
<p>If you're a professional services firm with 3 or more staff and you can name a task you or your team repeats every week that a system should be handling instead, that's usually enough signal. A second test: if you, the owner, are still the person everything routes through before anything gets done, that's the bottleneck the audit is built to find and remove. The free 5-minute scorecard is the lowest-friction way to check before committing to anything paid.</p>

<h3>Is my data safe during the assessment?</h3>
<p>The audit is POPIA-compliant and works at the process level, not the data level. It maps how your workflows move information, not the client records themselves — you're not required to hand over client files for the diagnostic to work.</p>

<figure class="article-inline-img">
  <img src="/images/article-audit-popia.png" alt="POPIA is not the obstacle to AI adoption. Bad implementation is — the audit looks at your workflows, not your files" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption>The Compliance Question</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>Do I have to implement everything the report recommends?</h3>
<p>No. The report is ranked specifically so you can start with the single highest-impact fix and stop there if that's all you want to do. Nothing in the deliverable requires a follow-on engagement.</p>

<h3>What if I already tried AI and it didn't stick?</h3>
<p>That's useful data, not a failed experiment to be embarrassed about. The audit's first job is explaining why the earlier attempt didn't hold — usually a tool applied to the wrong workflow, or a rollout with no one owning it — and prescribing differently based on that.</p>

<h3>Do you also do the implementation?</h3>
<p>Yes, as a separate, scoped engagement once you've seen the plan. It's optional. Some practices hand the day-by-day plan to an internal team member or existing IT contractor instead, and that's a legitimate way to use it.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
